{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"fd4757a1-aab6-4537-a4d0-7812ac6d05da","step":{"id":"s1","script":"These triangles have matching angles, so they are similar. Their shapes match even though their sizes differ.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Similar triangles"},{"op":"figure","id":"triangles","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-0.7,10.2],"y":[-0.7,6.7],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"a","at":[0,0],"label":"A"},{"kind":"point","id":"b","at":[3,0],"label":"B"},{"kind":"point","id":"c","at":[0,4],"label":"C"},{"kind":"point","id":"d","at":[5,0],"label":"D"},{"kind":"point","id":"e","at":[9.5,0],"label":"E"},{"kind":"point","id":"f","at":[5,6],"label":"F"},{"kind":"polyline","points":[{"at":"a"},{"at":"b"},{"at":"c"}],"closed":true},{"kind":"polyline","points":[{"at":"d"},{"at":"e"},{"at":"f"}],"closed":true},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"a"},"from":{"at":"b"},"to":{"at":"c"}},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"d"},"from":{"at":"e"},"to":{"at":"f"}},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"b"},"from":{"at":"c"},"to":{"at":"a"},"label":"θ"},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"e"},"from":{"at":"f"},"to":{"at":"d"},"label":"θ"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"fd4757a1-aab6-4537-a4d0-7812ac6d05da","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Compare corresponding sides: base with base, height with height. Both grow by the same scale factor, one point five.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"lengths","on":"triangles","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[1.5,-0.35],"text":"3"},{"kind":"text","at":[7.25,-0.35],"text":"4.5"},{"kind":"text","at":[-0.35,2],"text":"4"},{"kind":"text","at":[4.65,3],"text":"6"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"similarity","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\triangle ABC\\sim\\triangle DEF"},{"op":"equation","id":"scale","anchor":{"el":"similarity","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\frac{DE}{AB}=\\frac{DF}{AC}=\\frac{4.5}{3}=\\frac{6}{4}=1.5"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If the smaller triangle's hypotenuse is 5, what is the larger triangle's hypotenuse?","expected_gist":"7.5, because every corresponding side is multiplied by 1.5"}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"fd4757a1-aab6-4537-a4d0-7812ac6d05da","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The hypotenuse uses that same multiplier: five becomes seven point five. Similar triangles have equal ratios of corresponding sides.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"hypotenuses","on":"triangles","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[1.9,2.2],"text":"5"},{"kind":"text","at":[7.65,3.1],"text":"7.5"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"hypcalc","anchor":{"el":"scale","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"EF=1.5\\times5=7.5"},{"op":"equation","id":"proportion","anchor":{"el":"hypcalc","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\boxed{\\frac{DE}{AB}=\\frac{DF}{AC}=\\frac{EF}{BC}}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
